    <body>
      <sec id="article-28270.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>This continuing medical education activity provides clinicians with a comprehensive review of rectal prolapse in the pediatric population, with emphasis on the anatomic, etiologic, and clinical factors that distinguish this condition in children from its presentation in adults. Rectal prolapse is a frequently encountered pediatric concern that is often mismanaged due to insufficient recognition of its predisposing conditions, diagnostic criteria, and evidence-based treatment pathways. This activity addresses the gap between current clinical practice and established standards by reviewing classification, differential diagnosis, stepwise management, and indications for surgical referral. Participants will gain practical skills in manual reduction techniques, evaluating underlying causes, and coordinating interdisciplinary care. The activity emphasizes patient-centered outcomes, caregiver education, and strategies to reduce recurrence and prevent complications in affected children.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the anatomic and developmental characteristics that predispose infants and young children to rectal prolapse and distinguish these features from adult anatomy.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Assess pediatric patients presenting with rectal prolapse for underlying predisposing conditions, including cystic fibrosis, infectious diarrhea, constipation, and congenital anomalies.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select evidence-based treatment strategies for the underlying causes of rectal prolapse, including functional constipation, infectious diarrhea, and malabsorption syndromes.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with gastroenterologists, pediatric surgeons, nurses, and other members of the interprofessional team to develop coordinated, patient-centered care plans that support caregiver education and reduce the risk of recurrence.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=28270&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=28270">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28270.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Rectal prolapse refers to the extrusion of some or all of the rectal mucosa through the external anal sphincter (see <bold>Image.</bold>&#x000a0;Pediatric Rectal Prolapse). The condition most commonly occurs between infancy and 4 years of age, with peak incidence in the first year of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;In this population, rectal prolapse is usually a self-limiting condition, often resolving spontaneously or with conservative treatment of underlying contributing factors such as constipation or increased intra-abdominal pressure.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28270.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Rectal prolapse occurs more frequently in children because of developmental anatomic characteristics present in early life. In pediatric patients, the rectum is oriented more vertically along the sacrum and coccyx and lies relatively lower within the pelvis compared with other pelvic structures. The rectal mucosa is often redundant and only loosely connected to the underlying muscular layer. In addition, the sigmoid colon demonstrates greater mobility than in adults, and the levator ani muscle provides comparatively limited pelvic support. Structural support from the rectal valves of Houston is also incomplete in most infants younger than one year, further predisposing this population to prolapse.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;After age 4, the pelvic and rectal anatomy begins to resemble that of the adult rectum, thereby significantly reducing the incidence of rectal prolapse.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Predisposing Conditions</bold>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In older children, rectal prolapse should be regarded as a clinical sign that may indicate an underlying disorder contributing to its development.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Increased bowel motility:</bold> (infectious diarrhea&#x02014;amebiasis, giardiasis, trichuriasis, <italic toggle="yes">Salmonella</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">Shigella</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">Escherichia coli</italic> 0157:H7; and noninfectious causes such as ulcerative colitis and laxative abuse)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Increased abdominal pressure:</bold> chronic constipation, persistent coughing, recurrent vomiting, straining at urination</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Congenital conditions:</bold> cystic fibrosis, myelomeningocele, Hirschsprung disease, congenital hypothyroidism, spina bifida</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Miscellaneous:</bold> mucosal polyps/tumors, imperforate anus, post-surgical constipation, malnutrition&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r5">[5]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28270.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>In the pediatric population, RP occurs with equal frequency in boys and girls.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r7">[7]</xref> In contrast, adult populations show a markedly higher susceptibility in women, with an approximately 6-fold higher risk than in men. The incidence is influenced by geographic and socioeconomic factors. RP occurs more frequently in resource-limited settings, where parasitic infections, malnutrition, and diarrheal diseases are prevalent. In industrialized regions, common contributing factors include constipation, pertussis, cystic fibrosis, and colorectal polyps, with constipation representing the most frequent association in the United States. Although cystic fibrosis has been reported in up to one-fifth of affected children, the majority of children with rectal prolapse do not have previously unrecognized cystic fibrosis, particularly in the absence of suggestive symptoms.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28270.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The types of rectal prolapse include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Full-thickness rectal prolapse (procidentia)</bold>: A circumferential, full-thickness protrusion of the rectal wall through the anus, characterized by concentric folds of prolapsed tissue.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Mucosal prolapse</bold>: Involves only the rectal mucosa (not the full thickness of the rectal wall), characterized by radial invaginations rather than concentric folds; this has a more benign course and may be found in asymptomatic patients.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Internal rectal prolapse (intussusception)</bold>: A full-thickness intussusception that remains internal (intrarectal or intra-anal) and does not protrude externally; can only be demonstrated on defecography or proctoscopy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r8">[8]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28270.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Rectal prolapse commonly presents as a dark red, protruding mass (in approximately 70% of patients), which may be accompanied by mucus or bleeding (about 25%), particularly during straining.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r9">[9]</xref> Parents and caregivers are often the first to notice the protrusion and seek medical evaluation. The condition is generally painless, although some children may report mild discomfort. On digital rectal examination, anal sphincter tone may initially be reduced or absent, with recovery typically occurring within several hours. However, in children with underlying neurologic disorders, diminished sphincter tone may persist. Adolescents more frequently report symptoms such as tenesmus, anorectal pain, and the passage of blood or mucus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;In adolescents, these concerns warrant careful evaluation for underlying contributing conditions.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28270.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Diagnosis of rectal prolapse is based on history, and the mass is an obvious physical examination finding. However, because prolapse often resolves before the patient seeks medical attention, the parent's description of a mass that appeared during straining may provide sufficient information to establish the diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref> Occult rectal prolapse is often identified during sigmoidoscopy, which may demonstrate distal rectal erythema and mucosal granularity or reveal a polypoid, white-topped lesion along the anterior rectal wall.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Evaluation of Secondary Causes</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Screening for cystic fibrosis should be considered only in patients with recurrent rectal prolapse that does not respond to conservative&#x000a0;treatment and in whom no underlying cause has been identified.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r11">[11]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Colonoscopy can be performed if a solitary rectal ulcer or rectal polyps are suspected.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r10">[10]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Patients with constipation as the likely cause of their prolapse should receive contrast radiography of the colon and anorectal manometry.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r12">[12]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Evaluation for associated pelvic floor anomalies and further characterization of prolapse may also be performed by fluoroscopic dynamic defecography or MRI.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28270.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>The primary treatment goal for rectal prolapse is to diagnose and treat the predisposing condition.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Urgent Reduction of Rectal Prolapse</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Manual reduction</bold>
</p>
        <p>Manual reduction should be performed as soon as possible if the prolapse has not resolved spontaneously, because rectal prolapse will eventually become more difficult to reduce.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Manual reduction of rectal prolapse is performed with gloves, lubricant, gauze, and tape. Sedation and analgesia may be necessary depending on the size of the prolapse and the child's level of discomfort. The child is placed in the knee-chest position on an examination table or on the parent's lap, and the examiner's finger is inserted into the rectum to guide the prolapse reversal. The prolapse should reduce in 5 to 15 minutes. Sugar (113 g) or a 50% glucose solution can be applied if reduction is difficult, which acutely reduces swelling by osmotic fluid shift.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical reduction</bold>
</p>
        <p>If rectal prolapse cannot be reduced manually, urgent surgical consultation is warranted for reduction under sedation or general anesthesia.</p>
        <p>
<bold>After care</bold>
</p>
        <p>Parents and patients can be taught to manually reduce the prolapse if it recurs. Families should be provided with anticipatory guidance to reduce the risk of early recurrence and be advised of the importance of prompt reassessment if prolapse recurs. Patients should be sent home with a bowel regimen consisting of either stool softeners for constipation or avoidance of prolonged straining. The bowel regimen should continue until the child has regular bowel movements for several months without rectal prolapse.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Treatment of Underlying Causes</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Functional constipation</bold>
</p>
        <p>In young children with constipation and recurrent rectal prolapse despite treatment with osmotic agents such as polyethylene glycol, the addition of a stimulant laxative such as sennosides should be the next step in the bowel regimen. Treatment may be further escalated to bisacodyl as needed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Infectious diarrhea</bold>
</p>
        <p>Treatment should be guided by the identified pathogen, such as <italic toggle="yes">Shigella</italic> spp, <italic toggle="yes">Escherichia coli</italic>, or intestinal parasites, including <italic toggle="yes">Trichuris trichiura</italic>, enterobiasis (pinworm), and giardiasis. Empiric antibiotic therapy for suspected bacterial enteritis is generally not recommended.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Malabsorption syndromes</bold>
</p>
        <p>In malabsorption syndromes such as cystic fibrosis, celiac disease, and short bowel syndrome, treatment consists of fluid replacement and dietary changes; pharmacotherapy and pancreatic enzyme adjustments are used to prevent steatorrhea.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical management</bold>
</p>
        <p>Indications for pediatric surgery referral include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Refractory rectal prolapse</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Two or more episodes of rectal prolapse requiring manual reduction</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Persistent rectal pain</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Persistent rectal bleeding</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Perineal excoriation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Permanent pelvic floor weakness (eg, myelomeningocele, spinal cord injury, tethered cord)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Anatomic anomalies (lead points such as polyps, lymphoid hyperplasia, solitary rectal ulcer, or tumors [rare])</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Surgically corrected Hirschsprung disease or imperforate anus</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rectal-anal trauma (physical or sexual child abuse)<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r15">[15]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r16">[16]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Sclerotherapy</bold>
</p>
        <p>Submucosal injection of a sclerosant agent is considered a first-line treatment because of its simplicity and efficacy. The surgical procedure involves an injection above the dentate line. Sclerotherapy is generally well tolerated and associated with brief hospitalization, rapid recovery, and a low rate of complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical Procedures</bold>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>This approach is generally reserved for patients who do not respond to local sclerotherapy or is considered an initial option when sclerotherapy is unlikely to be effective.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Anal cerclage (Thiersch procedure): This surgical procedure consists of placing a circumferential suture at the anal opening.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rectopexy: Surgical treatment for complete&#x000a0;rectal prolapse (using sutures, mesh, talc, resection, or levatorplasty) is safe and effective, and is associated with improved functional outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r18">[18]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <table-wrap id="article-28270.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="5" cellspacing="2" cellpadding="20">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td style="width: 115.694px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pause and Reflect</td>
                <td style="width: 818.306px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>A toddler is brought to the clinic after a caregiver reports a recurrent, painless, protruding rectal mass during straining that typically reduces spontaneously but has recently required manual reduction. The child also has a history of chronic constipation.</p>
<p>How should the clinician respond in this situation, and what is the most appropriate next step in management to address both the acute presentation and the underlying cause while minimizing the risk of recurrence and complications?</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28270.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis, from&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">The Textbook of Pediatric Emergency Procedures,</italic> for rectal prolapse includes:&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Ileocecal intussusception: Though uncommon, a prolonged ileocolic intussusception may extend distally, allowing the intussuscepted bowel or appendix to protrude through the anus and mimic rectal prolapse. Patients may appear ill and experience episodic, severe abdominal pain, although they may seem relatively well between painful episodes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prolapsing rectal polyp or rectal hemorrhoids: Direct examination of the prolapsed tissue can help distinguish these conditions from&#x000a0;rectal prolapse</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Prolapsing rectal duplication cyst&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r19">[19]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28270.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The underlying cause largely determines outcomes in rectal prolapse. Nearly 90% of children younger than 4 years old improve with medical treatment alone and do not require surgical intervention. In contrast, about 10% experience persistent or recurrent prolapse that may continue into adulthood.&#x000a0;Children presenting after age 4 are more likely to have associated neurologic or pelvic musculoskeletal abnormalities, or chronic functional constipation, and tend to respond less favorably to conservative therapy. These children should be referred promptly for surgical evaluation and consideration of operative management. Recurrence beyond 6 years of age is uncommon.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r20">[20]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28270.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Local pain and self-limited mucosal bleeding are the most common complications in cases that reduce spontaneously or are manually reduced (from <italic toggle="yes">Nelson's Essentials of Pediatrics)</italic>. Prolonged rectal prolapse can lead to ulceration, venous obstruction, and thrombosis. Most complications, however, are associated with surgical management and can include bleeding, injury to the bladder neck, damage to the presacral nerves, and the development of a pelvic hematoma.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28270.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>While awaiting definitive management, caregivers should be instructed on proper techniques for manual reduction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-28270.r22">[22]</xref> After successful reduction, clinicians can teach parents, caregivers, and older patients how to manage recurrent episodes at home. Guidance on proper toileting posture, with adequate buttock support and minimized intra-abdominal pressure during defecation, should also be provided. Additional interventions should target the identified underlying cause.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28270.s13" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Rectal prolapse is the extrusion of rectal mucosa or the full rectal wall through the anal canal, most commonly affecting children younger than 4 years due to immature pelvic support structures and increased rectal mobility.&#x000a0;Rectal prolapse is&#x000a0;often self-limited and associated with conditions that increase intra-abdominal pressure or bowel motility, such as constipation, chronic cough, or infectious diarrhea. Clinically, it presents as a protruding, dark red mass with possible mucus or minor bleeding, typically during straining. Diagnosis is primarily clinical, though evaluation for underlying etiologies is essential in recurrent or atypical cases. Management focuses on prompt manual reduction and treatment of contributing conditions, with optimization of the bowel regimen playing a central role. Most cases resolve with conservative measures, while refractory or recurrent prolapse may require interventions such as sclerotherapy or surgical repair. Complications are generally mild but may include mucosal bleeding and, in prolonged cases, ulceration, venous obstruction, or thrombosis.</p>
        <p>Interprofessional collaboration is essential to optimize outcomes and prevent recurrence. Physicians and pediatric specialists lead diagnostic evaluation, identify underlying causes, and determine the need for procedural or surgical intervention, while primary care clinicians and advanced practitioners coordinate longitudinal care and monitor response to therapy. Nurses play a critical role in caregiver education, particularly in teaching manual reduction techniques and reinforcing adherence to bowel regimens. Pharmacists support safe and effective medication management, including laxatives or treatments for underlying conditions. Effective communication among team members ensures timely referral for refractory cases and reduces complications associated with delayed care. Shared decision-making with caregivers, anticipatory guidance, and coordinated follow-up enhance patient safety, improve adherence, and support long-term resolution through a systems-based, patient-centered approach.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-28270.s14">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=28270&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=28270">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
